--TEST--
A Core\Crypto\KeyPair written as PKCS#8 and read back agrees the secret the original pair agrees, on both curves and from either end — the private scalar survives the file, which a public half alone cannot show — `rule:core-classes/crypto-interop-tier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// What `->write` is for: a server keeps its pair across requests as one file
// and has nothing else afterwards. `crypto-generate-key-pair-answers-a-pair-read-takes-back.nvst`
// asserts that the file reads back as a pair with the same public half, and
// that is the half a PKCS#8 can simply *carry* — it is an optional field of
// the structure, so a read that dropped the scalar, kept a truncated one or
// re-derived the wrong one still answers the right public key and fails only
// when it is asked to agree. So the assertion here is the agreement itself,
// over both curves the roster agrees on, and from both ends of each.
array<Core\Crypto\KeyKind> $curves = [
    Core\Crypto\KeyKind::P256,
    Core\Crypto\KeyKind::X25519,
];

int $agreed = 0;
int $bothEnds = 0;
int $stable = 0;
int $separated = 0;
foreach ($curves as Core\Crypto\KeyKind $curve) {
    Core\Crypto\KeyPair $mine = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair($curve);
    Core\Crypto\KeyPair $peer = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair($curve);

    // The whole of what is kept: a `secret bytes` PKCS#8, read back with the
    // kind the program knows it stored.
    secret bytes $stored = $mine->write();
    Core\Crypto\KeyPair $restored = Core\Crypto\KeyPair::read($stored, $curve);

    if (Core\Crypto::agree($restored, $peer->publicKey())
        == Core\Crypto::agree($mine, $peer->publicKey())) {
        $agreed += 1;
    }

    // The peer's side of the same agreement, computed against the restored
    // pair's own public half: a scalar and a public key that no longer belong
    // together agree two different secrets here while agreeing with nothing.
    if (Core\Crypto::agree($peer, $restored->publicKey())
        == Core\Crypto::agree($restored, $peer->publicKey())) {
        $bothEnds += 1;
    }

    // Writing what was read is the same file, so a program that stores its
    // pair again after a restart stores what it had. `secret bytes` compares
    // against `secret bytes` with no reveal written anywhere in this case.
    if ($restored->write() == $stored) {
        $stable += 1;
    }

    // The control the three totals above need: agreement is not something any
    // two objects of this kind do. A second pair on the same curve agrees a
    // different secret with the same peer, so the equalities are about this
    // pair's scalar and not about the member answering a constant.
    Core\Crypto\KeyPair $other = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair($curve);
    if (Core\Crypto::agree($other, $peer->publicKey())
        == Core\Crypto::agree($mine, $peer->publicKey())) {
        echo "another pair agreed this pair's secret", "\n";
    } else {
        $separated += 1;
    }
}

uint $both = Core\Arr::count($curves);
echo "restored pairs agree the same secret ", $agreed, " of ", $both, "\n";
echo "and from the peer's end too ", $bothEnds, " of ", $both, "\n";
echo "the stored file is what is written back ", $stable, " of ", $both, "\n";
echo "another pair agrees another secret ", $separated, " of ", $both, "\n";
--EXPECT--
restored pairs agree the same secret 2 of 2
and from the peer's end too 2 of 2
the stored file is what is written back 2 of 2
another pair agrees another secret 2 of 2
